{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1479337-minimum-standards-for-combat-preparedness","id":1479337,"title":"Minimum Standards for Combat-Preparedness?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-04-23T07:26:07-07:00","id":1479337,"title":"Minimum Standards for Combat-Preparedness?","content":"Haven't played 2e yet, but I'm planning on running it sometime soon, most likely with players who also have zero experience of the system. I have run Scion 1e before, as well as a few other WW/OP systems.<br /> <br /> One of the things that I love about WW/OP games is that it's possible to create characters who aren't focused on combat. I have also learned (the hard way) that sometimes that can be a trap as combat may happen to your character whether you want it or not, and if your character isn't built at least a little bit for it, you can wind up having a bad time. In the past, I've found myself in more than one Exalted game where it was just understood that if your character doesn't have high soak and at least one perfect defense charm, you're pretty much doomed.<br /> <br /> I'm not a particularly harsh ST, and I don't expect my players to optimize their characters to take on my overpowered antagonists, but I do like to put them in sticky situations from time to time, and I don't want them to just straight up die because they didn't build their character to survive a basic low-level combat. Since my players will be new to the system, I'd really like to be able to give them some guidance as to how they can build their characters to survive (if not thrive in) standard combat situations. Save us all from unpleasant surprises.<br /> <br /> All of which is to preface the question: What does a starting Hero-level character need in order to survive a standard Scion game, assuming that they will, on occasion, be threatened with violence? All perspectives welcome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12108,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12108-gscrap","user":"gscrap","date":". 04-23-2022, 10:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12108-gscrap","name":"gscrap","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12108_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T13:52:35-07:00","id":1479369,"title":"","content":"So, first off, Scion 2e is a more &quot;meta&quot; game or a more &quot;narrative&quot; game depending on how you like your definitions. The players can opt to instantly check off their final health box and exit combat on the understanding that there will be consequences but death isn't one of them. The Band tries to fight a bunch of guards and the first round goes really shitty? They can all opt to get Taken Out, get captured, and deal with the fall out of that (plus the massive influx of Momentum they'll get for it will help resolve whatever comes next pretty handily).<br /> <br /> Characters have Tier ratings, and you get plot armor against worse Tiers than yourself. There is, quite literally, no way for certain enemies to roll well enough to kill the PCs unless the players and SG agree a PC death would be appropriate. The SG also has the power to declare any attack a &quot;fated&quot; attack and just delay the damage to some other time; the character now having a Sword of Damocles waiting to hit them.<br /> <br /> The core 2e system also limits characters of the same scale to 1 or 2 damage total per attack.<br /> <br /> The point being... it's kind of the exact opposite of a lot of the old WW games. The default assumption is that even if the PCs lose a fight, they're not going to die (or at least not die in a bad way... shedding mortality to become divine can be quite literal for high level Scions). Unlike Exalted (esp. 2e) where you have to work hard to make sure you don't accidentally squish your players if they're not built combat capable enough, Scion 2e makes it a lot of work to kill PCs if you follow the RAW and aren't throwing them into a fight with Zeus on the first session while they're still Heroes.<br /> <br /> Scion 2e combat is also geared around being cooperative to get the maximum results. With that damage cap, combat heavies tend to have a lot of successes to spend on things besides damage. The best thing to do with those? Set the enemy up so it's easier for the non-combat focused characters to help. Disarm your foe so your Bandmate can snatch that Relic sword away. Slam them into a tree so your friend can call on their power over plants to tangle the opponent up. Just knock them down so their Defense is lower. Etc.<br /> <br /> This can also go the other way. Combat as a broad options for non-combat characters: The Complicate Action. This encompasses anything a character might due to impart Complications on someone else in combat. This is pretty much the go-to move for non-attack actions that will still hinder the opposition. Can't punch through a piece of paper? You can still shine a bright light in someone's eyes.<br /> <br /> -------------<br /> <br /> So, beyond that, the basics of combat?<br /> <br /> 1a) Have one of your Resilience (does not have to be Stamina) Attributes high. That's your Defense roll. It doesn't matter which, so just get one of them to 4 or 5 (this should not be difficult with your starting resources).<br /> <br /> 1b) Consider getting a bit of armor (doesn't have to be Relic armor, but that's awesome if it makes sense), and try to compliment it to your Defense. Stamina based defenders get more out of Soft Armor to get both increased health from high Stamina and increase the difficulty of Inflict damage against them via Soft. Composure and Resolved based Defense might want to opt for the extra health Hard armors provide if their Stamina is low so they can stick in the fight longer if they do take a hit. Remember that while one source of armor can only be Hard or Soft, if you have multiple sources of armor you can stack a Hard and a Soft armor together to be really hard to injury.<br /> <br /> 2) The whole Storypath system is based on trying to get Enhancements. You need enough dice to roll one successes (5-6 dice is plenty on average), but after that Enhancements are worth way more (6 dice with +2e is generally going to beat 10 dice with +0e). Make sure you can get them for your go-to combat tricks even if that's not hitting people really hard. Look especially towards your Calling Knacks for situational Enhancements that rely more on circumstance (Guardians protecting others, Hunters going after designated prey, etc) rather than just ones that aid specific actions, and towards Bonds which create a general pool of Enhancements members of the Bond can use to aid each other (did I mention the game is very oriented around cooperativeness?).<br /> <br /> 3) Get to know the other PCs so you know their strengths and weaknesses. Use your Stunts and the Complicate action to their greatest impact by knowing what will help and what isn't. It's great if you can blind an enemy, but if your friend needs them to see for their power to work on the target, you probably should have spent your successes on something else. Yet again... teamwork is combat optimization in this system.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T15:10:30-07:00","id":1479374,"title":"","content":"The flip side of that is that non-combat builds can also contribute immensely to combat by using some of their usually not-combat abilities to generate enhancements that the fighters in the group can take advantage of. If played right, they can survive and prosper in the fight without directly engaging the enemy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T23:39:28-07:00","id":1479403,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1479337-minimum-standards-for-combat-preparedness?p=1479374#post1479374\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The flip side of that is that non-combat builds can also contribute immensely to combat by using some of their usually not-combat abilities to generate enhancements that the fighters in the group can take advantage of. If played right, they can survive and prosper in the fight without directly engaging the enemy.</div> </div> </div> </div>We got previous topic on<b> Non-Combatants in Scion 2E</b> - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1434114-2e-non-combatants-in-scion\">read it fully hear</a>.<br /> <br /> One of simpler and more elegant idea was<i> Non-Combatants ( Non-comb for short ) generating Enhancement Stunts for combatant players.</i> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <b>For example:</b> Non-Comb has zero dots in Firearms - but still can make 'Cheap Shots' on enemy and success of his goes to Comb(s) that use them as Enhancement on their proper attack rolls! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Or you can make 'Strange Incantation' to summon attention of enemies from other Comb(s). Or <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HcsRcpFm6nI\" target=\"_blank\">flirt with the dragon in combat</a>, to make it confuse. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 04-24-2022, 02:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}